        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe centralization into `OwnerRegion` is the right instinct \u2014 a single source of truth for the city\u2192region table and postcode ranges, with both validators delegating to `isPostcodeValid` and the mapper delegating to `regionOf`. That part is clean and additive. Two structural issues, though:\n\n1. **Dependency direction / placement of `OwnerRegion`.** The class lives in `rest.function.owner`, but `OwnerMapper` (in the `mapper` package) now reaches *up* into the REST-function layer to call it. That inverts the natural layering \u2014 REST functions should depend on mappers/model, not the other way around. The region table and postcode ranges are a domain concept (city\u2192region, valid ranges) shared by persistence-adjacent mapping *and* request validation, so it belongs in a neutral location both can depend on without a mapper importing from `rest.function.*` (e.g. alongside the `model`/owner domain, or a shared util package). As placed, a maintainer touching the mapper would be surprised to find it coupled to a REST pipeline package.\n\n2. **Fully-qualified reference instead of an import in `OwnerMapper`.** The call is written inline as `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.OwnerRegion.regionOf(...)`, which is inconsistent with the import style used elsewhere (and even with `ValidateOwner`, which adds a normal `import`). Fixing #1 and adding a proper import would make the call site read naturally. The FQN here is also a tell that the dependency direction felt off to the author.\n\nMinor (not blocking): the `postcode != null && !OwnerRegion.isPostcodeValid(...)` guard is repeated verbatim in both `ValidateOwner` (throws immediately) and `ValidateOwnerFields` (accumulates into `errors`). The shared logic is already in `OwnerRegion`, and the two call sites differ in error handling, so this duplication is acceptable \u2014 no change needed there."
